  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 137 PM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nA strong jet will have setup by Friday night. It will be the \nstrongest to our South, but it will generate southwesterly winds. We \nare expecting the Sangre De Cristo mountains to receive a hit of \naccumulating snowfall between 2 and 4 inches Sunday morning into the \nafternoon. Then, portions of the I-25 corridor, particularly near \nthe Palmer Divide, could receive some snowflakes Saturday afternoon \ninto evening. Cold air is in ample supply aloft, but a shallow moist \nlayer and limited forcing will mean significant accumulations are \nnot likely, but a brief dusting on non-heated surfaces can't be \nruled out. \n\nAfter our Saturday system, we anticipate cooler temperatures and \nlighter winds to be the pattern across the region, on account of the \ncold front passing and a high pressure behind it. \n\nLooking at the next work week, the forecast begins to diverge. Two \nmore precipitation events are in the cards, one Monday night into \nTuesday, and one on Thanksgiving. For the Mon-Tue system, the GFS is \nprojecting a more impactful event for our area than the ECMWF. If \nthe former verifies, the I-25 corridor could again see a dusting, \nespecially further north, and our Central Mountains could \norographically lift a modest hit of snow Tuesday morning for Lake \nand Chaffee Counties. That said, looking at the ensembles and \nprobabilistic forecasting, I am leaning towards the ECMWF solution \nfor Tuesday, where all we'd get are some overcast conditions.\n\nThe system on Thanksgiving looks less dicey than the ones before it, \nbut scattered precipitation is still possible, and warrants watching \ngiven the high volume of travel that will be occuring on \nThanksgiving.",
